think Read the operator card, the seed autonomous-loop rota, and the acute/circumflex/nordic corpora plus glyph_accent to place the next rung.
think Two pre-decided routes stood open: circumflex-by-composition (a third repeat of an economy already proven on the grave and the acute) versus the uppercase Nordic companions that CLOSE the Scandinavian capitals. Chose the latter -- Lindy-first crux-first: finishing a whole named set outweighs repeating a proven economy a third time.
think Designed the three capitals on the compressed rows-2..6 body the accented capitals use, leaving rows 0..1 for a mark: A ring wears a hollow ring above the compressed A; AE is a fused ligature (mark rows blank, a full crossbar on the middle row); O stroke is the compressed O with a diagonal slash inking the bowl center.
think Mirrored the lowercase font5x7_nordic guard structure exactly -- three constructions, each read by its own toothed guard (ring present + spanning + hollow, ligature mark-less + crossbarred, stroke mark-less + bowl-center inked), so a mistyped ring or lost crossbar reds by name.
obs GREEN on the first metal run, no red; ink 15..19 of 35 as predicted, encoded 135 bytes over raw 105. tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0, both files plain 7-bit ASCII (numeric codepoint literals -- the ascii-first exception for a font codec's glyph tables).
obs Honest scope: this closes the Scandinavian CAPITALS (A ring, AE, O stroke), not the whole uppercase Latin-1 set -- the Icelandic capitals (Eth, Thorn, Y acute) remain, exactly as the lowercase Nordic corpus preceded the lowercase Icelandic. No false "closes the set" claim.
